# Distinct multi-step numericals with explicit units and substitutions.
num(2,'Gross fixed investment is ₹420 crore. Opening inventories are ₹90 crore and closing inventories ₹120 crore. Depreciation is ₹70 crore. Find inventory investment, gross investment and net investment.','1. Inventory investment = 120 − 90 = ₹30 crore.\n2. Gross investment = 420 + 30 = ₹450 crore.\n3. Net investment = 450 − 70 = ₹380 crore.\nHeading to remember: Gross Investment, Net Investment and Depreciation.')
num(2,'A machine costs ₹12 lakh, has scrap value ₹2 lakh and an expected life of 5 years. Find annual depreciation. If annual gross investment is ₹7 lakh, find net investment.','Annual depreciation = (12 − 2)/5 = ₹2 lakh.\nNet investment = 7 − 2 = ₹5 lakh.',3)
num(3,'GDPMP = ₹8,000 crore; depreciation = ₹500 crore; indirect taxes = ₹700 crore; subsidies = ₹100 crore; factor income from abroad = ₹180 crore; factor income paid abroad = ₹260 crore. Find NIT, NFIA, NDPFC and national income.','NIT = 700 − 100 = ₹600 crore.\nNFIA = 180 − 260 = −₹80 crore.\nNDPFC = 8,000 − 500 − 600 = ₹6,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 6,900 − 80 = ₹6,820 crore.',6)
num(3,'National income is ₹6,200 crore, depreciation ₹300 crore, NIT ₹450 crore and NFIA −₹50 crore. Find GDPMP.','GDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA\n= 6,200 + 300 + 450 − (−50) = ₹7,000 crore.')
num(3,'GDPMP is ₹4,800 crore and GNPMP is ₹4,700 crore. Depreciation is ₹200 crore and NIT is ₹300 crore. Find NFIA and national income.','NFIA = GNPMP − GDPMP = 4,700 − 4,800 = −₹100 crore.\nNNPFC = GNPMP − Depreciation − NIT = 4,700 − 200 − 300 = ₹4,200 crore.')
num(3,'Nominal GDP is ₹7,200 crore and real GDP is ₹6,000 crore. Calculate the GDP deflator. If nominal GDP later becomes ₹8,100 crore with deflator 125, find real GDP.','GDP deflator = 7,200/6,000 × 100 = 120.\nLater real GDP = 8,100/125 × 100 = ₹6,480 crore.\nAn index of 125 means prices are 25% above the base-year level, not necessarily 25% above the immediately preceding year.')
num(3,'An economy produces only rice and cloth. Base prices are ₹20/kg and ₹50/metre. Current prices are ₹25/kg and ₹60/metre. Current quantities are 100 kg and 80 metres. Find nominal GDP, real GDP and the deflator.','Nominal GDP = 25 × 100 + 60 × 80 = ₹7,300.\nReal GDP = 20 × 100 + 50 × 80 = ₹6,000.\nDeflator = 7,300/6,000 × 100 = 121.67 (approximately).\nUse current quantities in both measures.',6)
num(3,'GDPFC = ₹5,200 crore; NDPFC = ₹4,900 crore; indirect taxes ₹200 crore; subsidies ₹260 crore; NFIA ₹80 crore. Find depreciation, GDPMP and NNPMP.','Depreciation = 5,200 − 4,900 = ₹300 crore.\nNIT = 200 − 260 = −₹60 crore.\nGDPMP = GDPFC + NIT = 5,200 − 60 = ₹5,140 crore.\nNNPMP = GDPMP − Depreciation + NFIA = 5,140 − 300 + 80 = ₹4,920 crore.',6)
num(4,'A firm has domestic sales ₹900 crore, exports ₹100 crore, opening stock ₹80 crore, closing stock ₹110 crore and intermediate purchases ₹430 crore. Find output and GVA MP. Then find NVA FC if depreciation is ₹50 crore and NIT ₹40 crore.','Total sales = 900 + 100 = ₹1,000 crore.\nChange in stock = 110 − 80 = ₹30 crore.\nOutput = 1,000 + 30 = ₹1,030 crore.\nGVA MP = 1,030 − 430 = ₹600 crore.\nNVA FC = 600 − 50 − 40 = ₹510 crore.',6)
num(4,'A farmer sells wheat for ₹100 to a mill; the mill sells flour for ₹170 to a bakery; the bakery sells bread for ₹280 to households. No other intermediate inputs exist. Find each producer’s value added and total GDP.','Farmer GVA = 100 − 0 = ₹100.\nMill GVA = 170 − 100 = ₹70.\nBakery GVA = 280 − 170 = ₹110.\nTotal = ₹280, equal to final bread value. Adding full sales gives ₹550 and double counts ₹270.')
num(4,'Wages in cash = ₹1,000 crore, wages in kind = ₹100 crore, employer social contributions = ₹80 crore, rent and royalty = ₹200 crore, interest on productive loans = ₹120 crore, corporate tax = ₹50 crore, dividends = ₹70 crore, retained earnings = ₹30 crore, mixed income = ₹250 crore and NFIA = −₹40 crore. Find national income.','Compensation = 1,000 + 100 + 80 = ₹1,180 crore.\nProfit = 50 + 70 + 30 = ₹150 crore.\nOperating surplus = 200 + 120 + 150 = ₹470 crore.\nNDPFC = 1,180 + 470 + 250 = ₹1,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 1,900 − 40 = ₹1,860 crore.',6)
num(4,'PFCE ₹1,200 crore; GFCE ₹400 crore; gross domestic fixed capital formation ₹500 crore; change in stock −₹20 crore; exports ₹180 crore; imports ₹230 crore; depreciation ₹100 crore; NIT ₹80 crore; NFIA ₹30 crore. Find GDPMP and national income.','GDCF = 500 − 20 = ₹480 crore.\nNet exports = 180 − 230 = −₹50 crore.\nGDPMP = 1,200 + 400 + 480 − 50 = ₹2,030 crore.\nNNPFC = 2,030 − 100 − 80 + 30 = ₹1,880 crore.',6)
num(4,'For an economy, total output ₹2,500 crore and intermediate consumption ₹700 crore. PFCE ₹800 crore, GFCE ₹300 crore, GDCF ₹600 crore, exports ₹250 crore and imports ₹150 crore. Show that production and expenditure methods agree.','Value-added method: GDPMP = 2,500 − 700 = ₹1,800 crore.\nExpenditure method: GDPMP = 800 + 300 + 600 + (250 − 150) = ₹1,800 crore.\nBoth measure the same final production from different sides. Do not add inventories separately when already included in GDCF.')
num(5,'Currency held by the public is ₹600 crore; demand deposits ₹900 crore; other deposits with RBI ₹20 crore; net time deposits ₹1,500 crore; post-office savings deposits ₹100 crore. Find M1, M2 and M3 under the textbook definitions.','M1 = 600 + 900 + 20 = ₹1,520 crore.\nM2 = 1,520 + 100 = ₹1,620 crore.\nM3 = 1,520 + 1,500 = ₹3,020 crore.\nM3 adds time deposits to M1, not to M2.')
num(6,'An initial deposit of ₹2,000 enters the banking system. The required reserve ratio is 10%, there are no excess reserves and every loan is redeposited. Find total deposits, additional credit and reserves in the final position.','Multiplier = 1/0.10 = 10.\nTotal deposits = 2,000 × 10 = ₹20,000.\nAdditional credit = 20,000 − 2,000 = ₹18,000.\nTotal reserves = 10% × 20,000 = ₹2,000.\nDistinguish total deposits from new credit.',6)
num(6,'Initial deposit is ₹1,000 and reserve ratio falls from 20% to 10%. Find the maximum total deposits before and after, and their increase.','Before: 1,000/0.20 = ₹5,000.\nAfter: 1,000/0.10 = ₹10,000.\nIncrease = ₹5,000.\nThis assumes the same initial reserves, full lending and complete redepositing.')
num(6,'A bank lends against collateral worth ₹80 lakh. If the margin rises from 20% to 35%, find the maximum loan at each margin and the reduction.','At 20% margin: loan = 80 × 0.80 = ₹64 lakh.\nAt 35% margin: loan = 80 × 0.65 = ₹52 lakh.\nReduction = ₹12 lakh.\nHigher margin restricts credit against unchanged security.')
num(7,'C = 100 + 0.8Y. At Y = ₹500 crore, find C, S, APC, APS, MPC and MPS.','C = 100 + 0.8 × 500 = ₹500 crore.\nS = 500 − 500 = 0.\nAPC = 500/500 = 1; APS = 0.\nMPC = 0.8; MPS = 0.2.\nThis is the break-even point, but not necessarily equilibrium.',6)
num(7,'Income rises from ₹400 crore to ₹600 crore and consumption rises from ₹360 crore to ₹510 crore. Find MPC and MPS. Find APC and APS at the new income.','ΔC = 150; ΔY = 200.\nMPC = 150/200 = 0.75; MPS = 0.25.\nAt Y = 600: APC = 510/600 = 0.85; S = 90; APS = 90/600 = 0.15.\nMPC + MPS = 1 and APC + APS = 1.')
num(7,'Saving function is S = −60 + 0.25Y. Derive consumption function and find break-even income. Calculate saving at Y = ₹160 crore.','C = Y − S = Y − (−60 + 0.25Y) = 60 + 0.75Y.\nAt break-even S = 0: 0.25Y = 60, so Y = ₹240 crore.\nAt Y = 160: S = −60 + 40 = −₹20 crore (dissaving).')
num(7,'At income ₹1,000 crore, APC is 0.9. If income rises by ₹200 crore and MPS is 0.25, find new consumption, saving, APC and APS.','Initial C = 0.9 × 1,000 = ₹900 crore.\nMPC = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75; ΔC = 0.75 × 200 = ₹150 crore.\nNew C = ₹1,050 crore; new Y = ₹1,200 crore; new S = ₹150 crore.\nNew APC = 1,050/1,200 = 0.875; APS = 0.125.',6)
num(8,'C = 80 + 0.75Y and autonomous investment is ₹120 crore. Determine equilibrium income, consumption and saving.','Y = C + I = 80 + 0.75Y + 120.\n0.25Y = 200 ⇒ Y* = ₹800 crore.\nC = 80 + 0.75 × 800 = ₹680 crore.\nS = 800 − 680 = ₹120 crore = I.\nCheck planned S = planned I.')
num(8,'S = −150 + 0.3Y and planned investment is ₹210 crore. Find equilibrium income. At Y = ₹1,000 crore, explain inventory adjustment.','Equilibrium: −150 + 0.3Y = 210 ⇒ 0.3Y = 360 ⇒ Y = ₹1,200 crore.\nAt Y = 1,000: planned S = ₹150 crore < I = ₹210 crore.\nAD exceeds output by ₹60 crore; inventories fall unexpectedly and firms expand production.',6)
num(8,'Investment rises by ₹80 crore and equilibrium income rises by ₹400 crore. Find multiplier, MPC, MPS and change in consumption.','k = 400/80 = 5.\nMPS = 1/5 = 0.2; MPC = 0.8.\nΔC = ΔY − ΔI = 400 − 80 = ₹320 crore.\nFinal additional saving is ₹80 crore.')
num(8,'MPC is 0.6 and investment falls by ₹50 crore. Calculate the final changes in income, consumption and saving.','k = 1/(1 − 0.6) = 2.5.\nΔY = 2.5 × (−50) = −₹125 crore.\nΔC = 0.6 × (−125) = −₹75 crore.\nΔS = −125 − (−75) = −₹50 crore = ΔI.')
num(8,'An investment injection of ₹100 crore occurs with MPC 0.8. State income generated in the first four rounds and the eventual total.','Rounds: ₹100 crore, ₹80 crore, ₹64 crore, ₹51.2 crore.\nFirst four rounds total = ₹295.2 crore.\nEventual total = 100/(1 − 0.8) = ₹500 crore.\nLater rounds continue; the first four do not equal the final multiplier effect.')
num(9,'C = 100 + 0.8Y, I = ₹200 crore and full-employment income is ₹2,000 crore. Find equilibrium income, the deflationary gap and the required increase in autonomous expenditure.','Y* = (100 + 200)/0.2 = ₹1,500 crore.\nAt Yf = 2,000: AD = 100 + 0.8 × 2,000 + 200 = ₹1,900 crore.\nDeflationary gap = 2,000 − 1,900 = ₹100 crore.\nIncome shortfall = ₹500 crore. With k = 5, required autonomous expenditure increase = 500/5 = ₹100 crore.',6)
num(9,'C = 120 + 0.75Y, I = ₹280 crore and Yf = ₹1,200 crore. Determine the demand situation and the autonomous-expenditure correction.','Notional Y* = (120 + 280)/0.25 = ₹1,600 crore, above capacity.\nAt Yf: AD = 120 + 0.75 × 1,200 + 280 = ₹1,300 crore.\nInflationary gap = 1,300 − 1,200 = ₹100 crore.\nReduce autonomous expenditure by ₹100 crore. Real output cannot rise indefinitely above full employment in this model.',6)
num(9,'Full-employment income is ₹2,400 crore; current equilibrium income is ₹1,800 crore; MPS is 0.25. Find multiplier and the autonomous investment increase needed for full employment.','k = 1/0.25 = 4.\nRequired ΔY = 2,400 − 1,800 = ₹600 crore.\nΔI = ΔY/k = 600/4 = ₹150 crore.\nThe expenditure gap is ₹150 crore, while the income gap is ₹600 crore.')
num(10,'Revenue receipts = ₹900 crore; revenue expenditure = ₹1,050 crore; capital expenditure = ₹450 crore; non-debt capital receipts = ₹100 crore; interest payments = ₹120 crore. Calculate revenue, fiscal and primary deficits.','Revenue deficit = 1,050 − 900 = ₹150 crore.\nTotal expenditure = 1,050 + 450 = ₹1,500 crore.\nFiscal deficit = 1,500 − 900 − 100 = ₹500 crore.\nPrimary deficit = 500 − 120 = ₹380 crore.',6)
num(10,'Total expenditure is ₹2,000 crore, fiscal deficit ₹500 crore, non-tax revenue ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹100 crore. Find tax revenue.','Fiscal deficit = TE − Tax revenue − Non-tax revenue − Non-debt capital receipts.\n500 = 2,000 − T − 200 − 100.\nT = 2,000 − 200 − 100 − 500 = ₹1,200 crore.')
num(10,'Revenue deficit is ₹80 crore, revenue expenditure ₹600 crore, fiscal deficit ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹50 crore. Find revenue receipts and capital expenditure.','Revenue receipts = RE − RD = 600 − 80 = ₹520 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 200 + 520 + 50 = ₹770 crore.\nCapital expenditure = 770 − 600 = ₹170 crore.')
num(10,'Fiscal deficit is ₹700 crore and primary deficit is ₹460 crore. Find interest payments. If revenue receipts are ₹1,000 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹150 crore, find total expenditure.','Interest payments = FD − PD = 700 − 460 = ₹240 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 700 + 1,000 + 150 = ₹1,850 crore.')
num(11,'The exchange rate changes from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$. A $500 import is bought at each rate. Find the rupee cost before and after, and identify the domestic currency movement.','Before: 500 × 80 = ₹40,000.\nAfter: 500 × 84 = ₹42,000.\nRupee cost rises by ₹2,000. The rupee depreciates under a flexible system.\nThe ₹/$ quote rises 5%; the rupee’s dollar value falls by (1 − 80/84) × 100 ≈ 4.76%.')
num(11,'Dollar demand is D = 200 − 2e and supply is S = 20 + e, where e is ₹/$ and quantities are arbitrary units. Find equilibrium. Demand then becomes D = 230 − 2e. Find the new rate and explain the movement.','Initially: 200 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 180 ⇒ e = ₹60/$; Q = 80.\nAfter shift: 230 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 210 ⇒ e = ₹70/$; Q = 90.\nGreater dollar demand raises ₹/$ and depreciates the rupee.',6)
num(11,'A central bank fixes the rate at ₹70/$. At this rate dollar demand is $100 million and supply is $75 million. Find the intervention needed and its reserve effect.','Excess dollar demand = 100 − 75 = $25 million.\nThe central bank sells $25 million from reserves to meet the shortage and defend the rate.\nIts foreign-exchange reserves decrease by $25 million. This is not a market-clearing rate without intervention.',3)
num(12,'Goods exports ₹600 crore, goods imports ₹800 crore, services receipts ₹250 crore, services payments ₹150 crore, factor income receipts ₹40 crore, factor income payments ₹70 crore, transfers received ₹90 crore and transfers paid ₹20 crore. Find trade and current-account balances.','Trade balance = 600 − 800 = −₹200 crore.\nNet services = 250 − 150 = ₹100 crore.\nNet factor income = 40 − 70 = −₹30 crore.\nNet transfers = 90 − 20 = ₹70 crore.\nCurrent-account balance = −200 + 100 − 30 + 70 = −₹60 crore.',6)
num(12,'An economy has a current-account deficit of ₹180 crore and autonomous net capital inflow of ₹130 crore. Find its autonomous BoP balance and reserve change if reserves provide the only financing.','Autonomous balance = −180 + 130 = −₹50 crore.\nA ₹50 crore deficit must be financed by drawing down official reserves.\nReserve assets decrease by ₹50 crore; the accommodating financing entry closes the accounting balance.')
num(12,'Exports of goods ₹900 crore, imports ₹850 crore, net services ₹100 crore, net factor income −₹70 crore, net transfers ₹20 crore and autonomous capital-account balance −₹60 crore. Find the current-account and overall autonomous balances.','Trade balance = 900 − 850 = ₹50 crore.\nCurrent account = 50 + 100 − 70 + 20 = ₹100 crore surplus.\nAutonomous overall balance = 100 − 60 = ₹40 crore surplus.\nIf absorbed solely into reserves, reserve assets rise by ₹40 crore.')
